Yes. Exposure is not just what enters the system. It is what × when × state × context × person.

A useful model is:

Effect = stimulus × timing × internal state × environment × prior history × repetition × meaning

The same experience can therefore be almost inert at one moment and highly influential at another. A joke heard while relaxed lands differently than the same joke during conflict; an idea encountered during adolescence can integrate differently than at 45; a cue paired repeatedly with excitement can acquire a different association than when paired with boredom or fear.

There are several mechanisms underneath that: state-dependent learning, context-dependent memory, attention/salience, reward prediction, sleep and consolidation, hormonal/circadian state, and sometimes memory reconsolidation when an existing memory is reactivated and updated.

So exposure has a kind of receptivity window:

person_t + environment_t + stimulus
→ interpretation_t
→ plastic change
→ person_t+1

And because person_t+1 is now different, the next identical exposure is not actually identical anymore.

That makes it recursive:

exposure₁ changes receiver
→ changed receiver encounters exposure₂
→ exposure₂ has different effect
→ ...

Which means frequency alone is a crude variable. Sequence, spacing, emotional state, developmental period, social setting, and what immediately precedes/follows the exposure can matter as much as dose.

For our framework I’d call it Temporal Receptivity Mapping: don’t ask only “What does this stimulus do?” Ask “To whom, in which state, in which environment, at what point in the trajectory, and what does it prepare the system to do next?”

That is much closer to how real learning, conditioning, therapy, advertising, ritual, education, and relationships actually operate.